            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is generally air tight, however we did identify a couple of key areas you will want to address:



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The hole through the wall right above the door that enters the garage (where the outside fresh air ductwork comes through &amp; into the fancoil above the laundry)



** The 5" chimney for the current boiler will be eliminated if you install a new high efficiency, direct vent propane combination boiler (see the Heating System section of my report)



Savings: For a nominal 10% reduction in overall air leakage heat loss, you could potentially save $ 90 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4 GJ,</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings>

The attic space above the 2nd floor of your house appears to have 8" (R28) of fibreglass batt insulation. You were interested to know the expected energy savings if you were add another minimum R22 to increase the total up to R50 ....... while ensuring you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting. The attic area above the 2nd floor represents 90% of all the attic space (the other 10% is the space above the 10' ceilings in the main floor front office).



Savings: By increasing the attic insulation above from R28 up to a minimum R50 in the attic space above the 2nd floor, this is estimated to save $ 197 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9 GJ, and qualify for a $ 112.50 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your foundation is comprised of a rear walkout basement that has a rather large heated "crawlspace" below. The foundation space is heated in the following two ways:



1. Some new in-floor radiant heating with R22 batts below that you are currently installing in the suspended basement floor.

2. From the fancoil located in the mechanical room, with heat ducting into the upper basement area and also into the lower crawlspace location.



The perimeter walls of the total height foundation (basement + crawl below) are comprised of:



1. Bare concrete walls (mostly in the lower crawlspace) that are mostly below grade but partially above grade

2. Concrete walls that have 1.5" (R7.5) of extruded polystyrene rigid board insulation on the interior face), also mostly below grade (front of house) but still a portion (left &amp; right sides) that are above grade.

3. There are wood frame construction walls that sit above all of the rear foundation concrete (including below into the crawlspace) and along a portion of the left &amp; right side walls). The wood frame walls in the basement area are 2x6@16" with R20 and some interior strapping + drywall. The wood frame walls visible in the lower crawlspace are 2x6@16" without any interior strapping or drywall.



As the wood frame construction walls are very similar in R-value to the upper living area walls of the Main and 2nd floors, I am focusing my attention on the concrete walls. From my calculations, these concrete walls represent about 47% of the total exterior wall area of the upper basement &amp; lower crawlspace combined. This 47% is also roughly split 50/50 between bare concrete &amp; concrete walls that have the interior R7.5.



I have modeled an increase to all of the concrete walls up to a total R24 (either using additional rigid foam board or perhaps spray foam insulation. NOTE: The green heading for this section will likely say to increase to R30 - but that is just a glitch in the "Report Generator Tool" - I am only modeling an increase to R24.



Savings: By increasing all of the concrete wall foundation area up to a minimum R24, you could potentially save $ 1,207 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 49 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 440.60 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modeled a combination heating &amp; hot water system for this house - please see the recommendation in the Heating System section of this report.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a combination oil boiler that also heats your domestic hot water, with a 182 litre external storage buffer tank. The boiler has a 6" flue vent &amp; there is a 5" combustion air supply (that your boiler needs for combustion), that has been bagged over &amp; taped off. The online specs for your Burnham V85W boiler indicate it had an original performance rating of 86.2% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), however it is likely operating at a lower level now (almost 20 years later). This boiler is capable of heating the following areas of your home:



1. The basement, via the new in-floor radiant heating with R22 batts below that you are currently installing.

2. The main floor + the basement, via the fancoil located in the mechanical room.

3. The 2nd floor, via the fancoil located above the laundry near the entry door into the garage.



In addition to the main boiler, you are also using a newer ComfortBilt HP22 pellet stove on the main floor that has a rated Higher Heating Value (HHV) of 73.3% efficiency. This pellet stove currently has a combustion air pipe directed down through the basement and into the "crawlspace area" located under the basement floor. Therefore, the pellet stove is drawing its' combustion air from inside the house (same as the current oil boiler).



There is also a BIS built in wood fireplace (original to the 2003 construction of the house) that is rarely used. It does appear to have a dedicated outside combustion air supply.



For the main oil combination boiler, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the 5" chimney or the 5" combustion air supply pipe. It will not be susceptible to back-drafting combustion fumes back into the house - see my Additional Comments section of this report, specifically about the "Depressurization Test" and "Air Tightness &amp; Ventilation". 



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 1,155 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 47 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood, the 3 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 10.0 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current oil boiler does draw its' combustion air from inside the house (ie: not direct vent), and the 5" combustion air supply pipe in the basement (that was intended to supply the boiler) has been sealed over ......... increasing the risk of back-drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors on all levels of your home, including the basement. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Air Tightness &amp; Ventilation



Let me start off by saying that your house was constructed to be generally very air tight and has very few (and very minor) air leakage locations that were highlighted previously in the Air Sealing section of this report. The current air tightness of your house is 1.89 Air Changes Per Hour at a negative pressure difference of 50 Pascals (1.55 ACH50). The current air tightness target of the City of Yellowknife for new house construction requires a builder to achieve a target of 1.5 ACH50, so your house from almost 20 years ago is doing pretty well !!



As we discussed, building science strives to "Build Tight &amp; Ventilate Right". Now, we'll talk about your house ventilation.



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood fan

        - 3 x bathroom fans

        - The clothes dryer



In addition to the above, there are two fancoil system (with zoned heat loops from the boiler) to heat all levels of your house and the fancoil units include a "fresh air supply pipe" from the outside of the house. This system is meant to draw in some outside fresh air (mixing it with the return air supply from the ductwork inside the house) and heating the combined air for distribution back into the house. Whenever an exhaust fan (listed above) is turned on, the house requires "makeup air" to replace what is being exhausted .......... so some "makeup air" will be drawn into the house via the same fresh air supply pipe to the fancoil.



Overall for your house, the current system seems to be working OK - there are no indications of excessive moisture buildup on windows and the house smells good on all levels. If you ever find that the house is just too dry inside, you could try installing an inline damper on the outside fresh air supply pipes to the fancoils so you can manually adjust it to be partially closed. Of course ......... this will likely result in a bit higher depressurization being created in your house when exhaust fans are being used. For this reason, I am recommending you consider upgrading your boiler to a new Energy Star high efficiency unit that is direct vent, where combustion spillage is not a concern (see the Heating System section of my report).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



